Part 8 — Initramfs (Initial RAM Filesystem)
The initramfs image is critical — it contains the minimum drivers and tools needed for the kernel to find and mount your root filesystem.
rockylinux
+1

8.1 — Inspect Initramfs Contents
￼
bash
# List everything inside the current initramfs
lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img | less

# Search for a specific module inside initramfs
lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img | grep ext4

# Check if a modprobe config was included
lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img | grep modprobe.d
8.2 — Rebuild Initramfs
￼
bash
# Force rebuild for the current kernel
sudo dracut --force /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img $(uname -r)

# Rebuild for ALL installed kernels
sudo dracut --regenerate-all --force
8.3 — Add a Driver to Initramfs
If you need a driver available at early boot:

￼
bash
# Edit dracut config
sudo vi /etc/dracut.conf.d/mydriver.conf
# Add: force_drivers+=" megaraid_sas "

# Rebuild
sudo dracut --force
